WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:07.712
It was Christmas Day, 1995.

00:00:07.754 --> 00:00:09.589
My brother Rich and I, we're up early,

00:00:09.589 --> 00:00:11.466
we're excited, it's Christmas.

00:00:11.508 --> 00:00:12.968
He rushed into my room

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:17.806
and in there my father had left
his IBM ThinkPad and a note.

00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:24.187
"This is so you don't wake up
your mother and me early.

00:00:25.021 --> 00:00:26.231
Merry Christmas."

00:00:26.231 --> 00:00:28.358
Next to it was a present.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.153
It was a video game,
a football game, FIFA '96.

00:00:32.195 --> 00:00:33.446
We tore it open,

00:00:33.488 --> 00:00:35.615
put it into that laptop
and we huddled around

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:38.201
and played the match to end all matches.

00:00:38.994 --> 00:00:42.539
We played so many more matches after that,

00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:45.834
but neither of us became
professional footballers.

00:00:45.875 --> 00:00:47.294
Richard?

00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:48.503
He's a lawyer.

00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:51.256
Me, I work with data

00:00:51.298 --> 00:00:55.427
to go and help organizations
get that competitive edge and win.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:00.557
Back in '95, that game
was just a video game.

00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:01.725
Nothing more.

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:05.687
Little did we know
that in the years to come

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:08.440
you’d be able to compete
in massive online competitions.

00:01:08.481 --> 00:01:11.484
More importantly, you’d be able
to go and connect with people

00:01:11.526 --> 00:01:13.737
around the world
that you've never met before

00:01:13.778 --> 00:01:16.406
and have a shared experience.

00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:19.784
And that's exactly what is happening.

00:01:20.243 --> 00:01:24.122
The lines between the physical
and the digital world are blurring.

00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:28.835
And we're entering into a new world
where we can start to dream bigger.

00:01:30.003 --> 00:01:31.880
A far cry from the '90s,

00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:34.466
video gaming has overtaken music

00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:38.178
as the most important
aspect of youth culture.

00:01:38.595 --> 00:01:40.180
I see this with my children.

00:01:40.221 --> 00:01:43.141
They're much more likely
to go and watch the latest video

00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:45.935
of someone playing Minecraft or Roblox

00:01:45.977 --> 00:01:49.272
than look up what the UK
latest number one single is.

00:01:50.231 --> 00:01:54.944
And this has led to a shift
in how we consume entertainment.

00:01:55.528 --> 00:01:57.822
We are no longer consumers of it.

00:01:57.864 --> 00:02:02.535
We are now being shared participants
in an online experience.

00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:05.747
I myself love gaming.

00:02:05.747 --> 00:02:08.083
Not particularly good at it,
but I love it.

00:02:08.124 --> 00:02:10.210
And I love the Formula 1 game.

00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:13.672
And that’s because I love real-world
Formula 1 racing.

00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:15.298
I'm going to show you a clip why.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.508
So this is Lando Norris,

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:19.177
one of McLaren’s Formula 1 drivers.

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:23.598
Multiple podium wins last year
driving the MCL35M --

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.602
oh, MCL36, sorry --

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:29.771
the car for this year
in pre-season testing.

00:02:29.771 --> 00:02:31.147
Let's have a look at this.

00:02:31.564 --> 00:02:34.859
(Car engine revving)

00:02:41.658 --> 00:02:44.494
Isn't the noise of one of those
just incredible?

00:02:45.662 --> 00:02:47.580
But the atmosphere, the drama,

00:02:47.622 --> 00:02:49.666
the suspense of going
to one of these races

00:02:49.708 --> 00:02:52.752
and watching those cars
go around, over 200 miles an hour,

00:02:52.752 --> 00:02:53.920
it's electric.

00:02:54.671 --> 00:02:58.466
But few of us will ever get
to sit in one of those cars,

00:02:58.508 --> 00:03:01.136
let alone be one of those
drivers on the grid.

00:03:01.428 --> 00:03:06.099
And that’s because in Formula 1,
there are only 20 professional drivers.

00:03:06.141 --> 00:03:09.477
There are ten teams
with two drivers per team.

00:03:10.228 --> 00:03:13.732
It's perhaps the most inaccessible
sport in the world.

00:03:13.773 --> 00:03:16.860
Suffice to say, there are going to be
so many people with dreams,

00:03:16.901 --> 00:03:21.239
talent and ambition to get there
who will never be able to make it.

00:03:22.365 --> 00:03:25.034
I'm going to introduce you
to someone called Rudy.

00:03:25.034 --> 00:03:26.244
Rudy Van Buren.

00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:29.539
He grew up in the Netherlands,
next to a go-karting track.

00:03:29.539 --> 00:03:31.541
At the age of eight,

00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:34.586
he got given a birthday
present, a go-cart.

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:36.254
He was fast.

00:03:36.254 --> 00:03:39.466
He was so fast, that in 2003,

00:03:39.507 --> 00:03:42.802
Rudy won the Dutch national championships.

00:03:42.802 --> 00:03:44.304
He had dreams and aspirations

00:03:44.345 --> 00:03:46.848
of becoming a professional
Formula 1 driver.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.141
Then at 16,

00:03:48.141 --> 00:03:52.937
like so many people that are trying
to make that next step in the sport,

00:03:52.979 --> 00:03:54.689
it just became too expensive.

00:03:54.731 --> 00:03:57.609
And that dream for Rudy was over.

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:02.489
I'm going to come back to Rudy later.

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:05.283
Times change, and so does technology.

00:04:05.742 --> 00:04:09.537
Dreams are being fulfilled
in a world of online gaming,

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:12.665
especially in this class of competition
called esports.

00:04:13.124 --> 00:04:16.795
Esports, at its simplest,
is digital competition

00:04:16.836 --> 00:04:22.217
where you can get real-world
and virtual prizes, fame and glory.

00:04:23.051 --> 00:04:25.720
There will always be spectators,

00:04:25.762 --> 00:04:29.516
in filling stadiums with spectators,

00:04:29.557 --> 00:04:31.935
athletes, fans.

00:04:31.976 --> 00:04:38.358
But now there are arenas
filled with die-hard fans

00:04:38.399 --> 00:04:39.943
with gaming rigs

00:04:39.943 --> 00:04:43.196
and a new class of elite competitor.

00:04:44.572 --> 00:04:47.408
Most importantly, it's accessible.

00:04:48.243 --> 00:04:51.412
Any one of us with a PlayStation, an Xbox,

00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:54.666
a PC with a 100-dollar steering wheel

00:04:54.707 --> 00:04:57.752
can go and get involved
in the thrill of competition.

00:04:57.794 --> 00:05:00.255
More importantly, you can go
and connect with people

00:05:00.296 --> 00:05:02.465
and share an online experience.

00:05:03.341 --> 00:05:05.343
There are so many
different types of games.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:08.012
Battle games, strategy games,

00:05:08.054 --> 00:05:11.724
football games -- soccer --
or racing games.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:15.812
The reason why I like racing
so much, it's unique.

00:05:15.854 --> 00:05:21.860
It is the only digital sport
that represents the real world so much.

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:25.989
It is almost an exact twin
of the physical world.

00:05:27.156 --> 00:05:29.033
As part of my day job,

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:31.578
I work with organizations
around the world.

00:05:31.578 --> 00:05:34.163
I work for a software company
specialized in data.

00:05:34.998 --> 00:05:38.418
My favorite organization I work with
at the moment is McLaren Racing.

00:05:39.168 --> 00:05:43.381
McLaren, founded in 1963
by the New Zealander Bruce McLaren.

00:05:43.882 --> 00:05:47.802
They’ve got an IndyCar team,
they have an extreme e-team.

00:05:47.844 --> 00:05:50.597
They have my personal favorite,
the Formula 1 team.

00:05:50.638 --> 00:05:53.892
Just look at that livery over there,
I think it's incredible.

00:05:54.642 --> 00:05:56.311
But Bruce was an innovator.

00:05:56.352 --> 00:06:00.398
In 1963, Bruce would have never imagined

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.026
that there would be
a professional gaming team,

00:06:03.026 --> 00:06:05.403
the McLaren Shadow esports team,

00:06:06.237 --> 00:06:07.614
That's their car.

00:06:08.823 --> 00:06:12.201
These two cars are almost
indistinguishable from each other.

00:06:12.243 --> 00:06:14.537
They're mirroring each other
in the physical

00:06:14.579 --> 00:06:16.414
and the digital world of sports.

00:06:17.123 --> 00:06:18.458
I spent time with Lando,

00:06:18.458 --> 00:06:21.252
one of those drivers,
talking about gaming.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.379
Lando is saying that the game,

00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:26.090
not the state-of-the-art simulators
that the McLaren have,

00:06:26.132 --> 00:06:27.342
the game,

00:06:27.383 --> 00:06:31.721
is so realistic that he can tell
if a bump in the road is out of place.

00:06:31.763 --> 00:06:34.974
The only thing that he feels
is missing is the wind.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:40.772
Lando is one of these digital natives,
a new class of gamer,

00:06:40.813 --> 00:06:45.401
where he drives in both the physical
world and the digital world

00:06:45.443 --> 00:06:47.320
in that shared community.

00:06:48.363 --> 00:06:52.575
The difference in Formula 1
between coming first and second

00:06:52.617 --> 00:06:55.286
is a fraction of a second.

00:06:56.079 --> 00:06:59.540
When you have elite people,
those 20 people in that car,

00:06:59.582 --> 00:07:03.002
the only way they can go
and get better is by data.

00:07:03.044 --> 00:07:05.546
It's the only way
they can differentiate themselves,

00:07:05.546 --> 00:07:07.423
using data to improve.

00:07:07.465 --> 00:07:10.760
This is exactly the same as esports.

00:07:11.094 --> 00:07:14.430
There are team managers,
there are coaches, even psychologists,

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.808
but most importantly, data analysts.

00:07:17.517 --> 00:07:20.853
In the racing game, the PC game,

00:07:20.895 --> 00:07:25.984
they hit out around 300
different data points in real time

00:07:25.984 --> 00:07:28.403
for you to analyze
and improve what you do.

00:07:28.444 --> 00:07:29.612
Not real F1.

00:07:29.612 --> 00:07:30.947
This is the game.

00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:34.784
Things like tire temperature,
brakes, acceleration,

00:07:34.826 --> 00:07:36.619
it's absolutely incredible.

00:07:36.953 --> 00:07:39.497
It's also incredibly realistic.

00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:40.707
Look at this.

00:07:40.748 --> 00:07:44.127
Sometimes you can look at this and think
that could be real life.

00:07:45.086 --> 00:07:47.463
It's an immersive experience.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.675
When you're going down
that, kind of the back straight,

00:07:50.675 --> 00:07:53.803
you're traveling about
293 feet per second.

00:07:54.137 --> 00:07:57.849
The difference between making that corner

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.059
and dropping back
and not being in the lead

00:08:00.101 --> 00:08:02.145
is a mere fraction of a second.

00:08:02.478 --> 00:08:05.648
You want to know: Are your tires
at the right temperature?

00:08:05.648 --> 00:08:07.025
You want to be able to know:

00:08:07.025 --> 00:08:09.235
Are you hitting the brakes
at the right moment,

00:08:09.235 --> 00:08:10.653
turning at that right moment?

00:08:10.653 --> 00:08:13.698
They're using data
to improve again and again.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.199
Here's Lando playing.

00:08:16.576 --> 00:08:19.537
Lando would be playing
again over lockdown.

00:08:19.579 --> 00:08:21.289
There was no Formula 1.

00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:22.999
Lando ended up gaming.

00:08:23.041 --> 00:08:24.876
The most empowering part about this,

00:08:24.917 --> 00:08:27.670
it wasn't just he was using this
to practice his skill.

00:08:28.212 --> 00:08:31.257
It was he was connecting
with an online community,

00:08:31.299 --> 00:08:37.138
an absolutely unprecedented access
to Lando, the sports person.

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:39.140
Something you don't normally get.

00:08:40.725 --> 00:08:43.102
Over lockdown, there was no sport.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:47.315
Journalists started writing
about esports.

00:08:47.774 --> 00:08:52.528
Mainstream broadcasters started
broadcasting esports on primary channels.

00:08:52.904 --> 00:08:56.074
We started looking at esports
in the millions.

00:08:56.491 --> 00:08:57.867
It was sport.

00:08:58.618 --> 00:09:00.578
It was the only sport.

00:09:01.579 --> 00:09:02.747
People under 20

00:09:02.747 --> 00:09:06.793
are much more likely to start
to watch esports or watch esports,

00:09:06.793 --> 00:09:10.421
than go and have a look
at traditional sports.

00:09:10.463 --> 00:09:12.799
The Commonwealth Games this summer

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.302
is going to have esports
alongside traditional sports.

00:09:16.344 --> 00:09:20.139
The Asian Games later this year
are going to have it as a medal event

00:09:20.139 --> 00:09:22.517
alongside traditional sports.

00:09:22.558 --> 00:09:26.395
The gaming industry is three times
bigger than the music industry.

00:09:26.437 --> 00:09:29.107
It's going to double by 2025.

00:09:29.982 --> 00:09:31.651
The most important part about it

00:09:31.692 --> 00:09:35.613
is it's redefining our notion
of what is sports and competition

00:09:35.613 --> 00:09:39.534
and how we get into
that accessible world of fun,

00:09:39.534 --> 00:09:43.037
of connecting with people
and enjoying ourselves.

00:09:43.913 --> 00:09:45.915
Now, I mentioned Rudy earlier.

00:09:46.249 --> 00:09:48.417
For Rudy, his dream hit a wall.

00:09:48.835 --> 00:09:50.503
He started gaming.

00:09:50.545 --> 00:09:51.838
He really enjoyed it.

00:09:51.879 --> 00:09:55.133
Then when he was 25, he saw a competition:

00:09:55.133 --> 00:09:56.843
World's Fastest Gamer.

00:09:57.301 --> 00:10:00.471
30,000 people entered this.

00:10:01.389 --> 00:10:02.557
Rudy won.

00:10:03.474 --> 00:10:08.563
Rudy got a professional contract
with the McLaren simulator team.

00:10:08.563 --> 00:10:12.150
This led on to Rudy getting
a contract for Huber racing

00:10:12.191 --> 00:10:15.611
in the Porsche Carrera Cup Germany
and the Super GT Cup.

00:10:15.653 --> 00:10:19.157
Rudy is winning some of the most difficult
competitions in the world.

00:10:20.199 --> 00:10:22.493
Just like he did in the virtual world.

00:10:23.035 --> 00:10:26.080
Play gives us new possibilities.

00:10:26.122 --> 00:10:28.875
The technology is closing the gap

00:10:28.916 --> 00:10:32.211
between our aspirations
and our access to that opportunity.

00:10:32.587 --> 00:10:34.630
It's closing down barriers,

00:10:34.672 --> 00:10:37.592
geographic barriers, social barriers,

00:10:37.592 --> 00:10:38.759
economic barriers.

00:10:38.801 --> 00:10:41.137
It enabled Rudy to go and live his dream.

00:10:41.179 --> 00:10:42.638
We all have dreams

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:45.600
and this is allowing us
to go and fulfill our dreams.

00:10:46.434 --> 00:10:50.396
A new generation of people
is starting to understand

00:10:50.438 --> 00:10:54.317
what it really means to go and live
in a world augmented by technology.

00:10:54.859 --> 00:10:57.695
The relevance of it, the context of it,

00:10:57.737 --> 00:11:00.323
what it actually means to go
and connect with people

00:11:00.364 --> 00:11:02.491
and technology being part of everything.

00:11:02.533 --> 00:11:05.786
They are able to go and reimagine
the world around them.

00:11:05.828 --> 00:11:07.914
They’re more data literate than ever.

00:11:08.539 --> 00:11:12.084
They're ready to go and create
that next wave of innovation.

00:11:12.752 --> 00:11:16.339
This really is the new era for play.

00:11:16.964 --> 00:11:18.716
And it's open to everyone.

00:11:19.508 --> 00:11:20.635
Thank you.

00:11:20.676 --> 00:11:25.681
(Applause)

